[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Integer Overflow/Underflow and Precision Loss in Blockchain"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Integer overflow and underflow, alongside precision loss, represent critical vulnerabilities in smart contract development. Because blockchain state modifications are immutable and often handle high-value assets, arithmetic errors can lead to catastrophic financial losses."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Integer Overflow and Underflow?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An integer overflow occurs when an arithmetic operation attempts to create a numeric value that exceeds the maximum limit of the integer type. Conversely, an underflow occurs when a value drops below the minimum limit. In languages like Solidity (versions prior to 0.8.0), a uint256 variable can hold values from 0 up to (2^256) - 1. If you add 1 to the maximum value, it wraps around to 0."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pragma solidity ^0.7.0;

contract OverflowExample {
    uint8 public count = 255;

    function increment() public {
        count += 1; // This will wrap around to 0 due to overflow
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Precision Loss in Blockchain"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Precision loss typically happens during division operations or when mixing units of different decimal scales. Because EVM (Ethereum Virtual Machine) does not support floating-point arithmetic, all decimals must be represented using large integers scaled by factors of 10 (such as 10^18 for Wei in Ethereum)."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// Example of precision loss due to premature division
function calculateShare(uint256 amount) public pure returns (uint256) {
    // Dividing before multiplying discards the remainder immediately
    return (amount / 3) * 2;
}

// Correct approach: Multiply before dividing
function calculateShareCorrect(uint256 amount) public pure returns (uint256) {
    return (amount * 2) / 3;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Mitigation Strategies"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Solidity 0.8.0 and above, which includes built-in overflow and underflow checks by default for all arithmetic operations."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Utilize audited math libraries such as OpenZeppelin's SafeMath for older versions of Solidity."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always multiply before you divide to minimize precision loss in token calculations."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conduct thorough fuzz testing and static analysis using tools like Slither and Echidna."}]
